From 2b0dbc186f503b918461a2e7528e1e15f2f112c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 09:52:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Draw a television's picture behind whatever the game drew in
 front of it

A screen's page is composited after the game has finished its frame, into the
presented image, by an overlay pass with no depth of its own. So the picture was
painted over everything between the camera and the set: V's hands and weapon,
V's body in third person, people walking past, the door frame it was seen
through. The producer's one ray to the screen's centre could only hide a whole
screen, and hands and bodies are not in the physics scene it asks.

The game's own depth buffer knows what is in front at every pixel. SceneDepth
finds it and the screen is drawn against it:

  * Finding it. From plugin load, WebUiService::Initialize hooks
    D3D12CreateDevice through RED4ext; when the game makes its device, the
    runtime's own device, command list and queue tables are hooked (only the
    runtime's: a wrapper's calls end up in them, so hooking both would count
    twice). Nothing the game does is changed. Per command list it notes which
    depth view is bound and how many draws and vertices go into it, every state
    change of a depth buffer, its clears and viewports; at ExecuteCommandLists the
    lists are merged in the order the GPU runs them. At present the buffer that
    took the scene's work among those shaped like the picture is the scene's --
    ReShade's generic-depth rule, which works on this game, widened to an
    upscaler's third-size depth (SceneDepthPolicy::Rank). Shadow maps are square
    and an interface stencil does little work, so neither is taken.
  * Copying it. In WebUiService::PrepareFrame, on the overlay's own command list,
    the buffer is moved from the state the game left it in (tracked from its
    creation and every barrier since) to COPY_SOURCE, copied whole into a texture
    of the overlay's, and put back exactly as it was. The copy is made only while
    a screen has been drawn in the last 120 presents. A frame whose buffer's
    memory was aliased away after its last draw is not copied.
  * What its values mean. Every perspective projection stores device = A + B / z;
    A follows from the clear value (0: reversed), and B, the near plane, is
    measured from the screens themselves: the producer publishes each screen's
    centre view depth, so wherever the game drew the set's own glass, device
    depth times the plane's view depth is B, and anything in front only makes
    that product larger. Nine points in the middle of each screen are read back
    every third present; the smallest product per screen, the median over many,
    locks once a window agrees within 4 %, and then moves only when a whole
    window agrees on another value, so a hand held over a screen for a few
    seconds cannot drag it (SceneDepthPolicy::Calibration). B is used 1.5 % large,
    which can only make the game's geometry look further away.
  * Drawing. DrawSurfaceQuad takes the centre's view depth (blended with the
    corners in WorldOverlay, so it moves with them) and queues the depth pass
    (ScreenDepthPass, ScreenDepth.hlsli) as an ImGui callback: one draw per
    screen, the exact per-pixel inverse of the corners' homography, and the scene
    tested four texels at a time with 5 cm (or 1.5 % of the distance) of
    tolerance for the prop's own glass and 3 cm of softness. Until a buffer is
    found the screen is drawn exactly as before; until B is measured, in
    perspective with the test off.

In game, Ctrl+Shift+F10 shows the buffer in use and the candidates, F11 turns
the test off and on, F9 tries the next candidate. OP77_TV_DEPTH=0 hooks
nothing. Everything is logged with the prefix "TV depth:", with a status line
every 30 s while screens are drawn.

Tests:

  * Open77.ScreenDepth.Tests -- the shader's own arithmetic, compiled as C++,
    against a ray-traced scene: ten scenes, three depth conventions, upscaler and
    dynamic-resolution depth, the tolerance shown to be necessary.
  * Open77.SceneDepthPolicy.Tests -- the buffer choice (a frame's shadow maps,
    scene, interface stencil and a half-size buffer; indirect draws; stickiness;
    the next-candidate key) and the calibration (a hand over some points, the
    sky, a hand held over the whole middle, a real change of near plane,
    conventional depth, noise that never locks).
  * Open77.ScreenDepthShaderSource.Tests -- the shaders embedded for D3DCompile
    are the files beside them.

Run end to end on Wine's Direct3D 12 (vkd3d on Mesa's software Vulkan) in the
open77-tv repository: a stand-in game draws a shadow map and a scene (a wall, a
television, a hand in front of it) at half resolution through the hooked
runtime, with the hooks installed at device creation and after it, and D32 and
D32_S8 depth; the overlay draws through ImGui's DX12 backend. In all four the
scene's buffer is chosen, B is measured as the near plane, and the last frame has
every pixel of the picture shown in the open and hidden behind the hand. Not run
on Windows, on a GPU driver, or in the game.

